  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["J1", "J2"],
      "pins": ["J1.25", "J1.27", "J2.27", "J2.25"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "PIN25"],
      "observed": "On the bottom M5Stack bus connector J2 the +5V global label is attached to pin 27 (pin 25 carries the spare net 'PIN25'), but on the top connector J1 the +5V label is attached to pin 25 and pin 27 is left unlabeled. The two supposedly identical pass-through connectors therefore map +5V to different header pins.",
      "expected": "Spec §2/§4: 'identical nets on both so the board can be stacked between the M5Stack Core and a lower module (pass-through)' and 'The same +5V net is also tied directly to J1/J2 pin 27 (the M5Stack bus's 5V pin)'. Both J1 and J2 pin 27 must carry +5V.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "J1 (Conn_02x15_Odd_Even) left column bottom: ...PIN23, +5V (at the pin-25 row), BAT; pin 27 has no net label. J2 left column bottom: ...PIN23, PIN25, +5V (at the pin-27 row), BAT."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "The same +5V net is also tied directly to J1/J2 pin 27 (the M5Stack bus's 5V pin)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The M5Stack Core supplies 5 V on bus pin 27, which is now unconnected on J1, so the board's +5V rail is not powered from the Core (only from the external 5 V input through D1). Meanwhile the Core's pin 25 is shorted to the board's +5V rail (and to the servo/PortB 5 V), which on the M5Stack bus is not the 5 V pin (likely GND/a spare), risking a 5 V-to-GND short through the Core and breaking the 5 V pass-through to any module stacked below J2.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "All component values, part types and the PWM/serial jumper logic match the specification, but the +5V global label is placed on J1 pin 25 instead of pin 27, breaking the J1/J2 pass-through symmetry and mis-connecting the M5Stack 5 V rail; this is the only deviation the drawing supports."
}

